WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:12.000
Delivery through water is different from crossing the plasma membrane. Nitrate can reach the membrane without crossing it.

00:12.000 --> 00:26.000
The lipid core restricts ion crossing. Small non-polar molecules such as CO2 can cross directly; ions usually need proteins.

00:26.000 --> 00:36.000
For an ion, concentration and voltage both matter. Nitrate is more concentrated outside, but a negative interior opposes entry of this negative ion.

00:36.000 --> 00:46.000
The total electrochemical energy change determines the favoured direction. A favourable direction still requires a permeable route and does not determine the rate.

00:46.000 --> 01:04.000
Protein-assisted downhill transport is passive. A generic carrier binds solute outside, encloses it, opens inside and releases it. Both sides are never open together.

01:04.000 --> 01:12.000
Primary active transport: ATP hydrolysis drives a proton pump. Protons are exported from the cytosol.

01:12.000 --> 01:20.000
Secondary active transport: returning protons help drive nitrate inward through a separate symporter.

01:20.000 --> 01:28.000
Symport means the coupled substances move in the same direction through the same protein. The separate pump and symporter do not form an antiporter.

01:28.000 --> 01:44.000
In this illustrative example, nitrate entry costs 19.13 kilojoules per mole. Entry of two protons releases 26.85. The combined inward cycle lowers free energy by 7.72.

01:44.000 --> 01:51.000
Stopping the pump does not instantly erase the gradient. The remaining gradient can initially support coupled uptake; that driving force is lost as the gradient dissipates.

01:51.000 --> 02:00.000
In this counterfactual, reverse coupling becomes favourable. The relaxation time is chosen for teaching, and the actual transport rate is not calculated.

02:00.000 --> 02:07.000
Does a saturating curve prove active transport? Consider two carriers with finite capacity.

02:07.000 --> 02:16.000
No. Passive facilitated transport can also saturate. Test the electrochemical gradients and coupling; curve shape alone is insufficient.
